2782a709-0852-4748-8fc7-bee559808950	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	0	Lesson 1	Page 1	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	LEARNSYNC: A Cloud-Based AI Academic Assistant for Student\nPerformance Development\nA Capstone Project\nPresented to the Faculty of the\nCOLLEGE OF INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY\nCENTRAL BICOL STATE UNIVERSITY OF AGRICULTURE – SIPOCOT\nSipocot, Camarines Sur\nIn Partial Fulfillment of the Requirements\nFor the Degree of\nBACHELOR OF SCIENCE IN INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY\nMOHAMMAD HASSAN L. DE MESA\nKRISSCEL A. MERENCIANO\nLEA JEAN C. CAMAING
74e59ef6-65a5-46f5-974a-432dc1ef0727	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	1	Lesson 1	Page 2	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	1\nINTRODUCTION\nLearning management systems have become essential in supporting the\ndelivery of instructional materials, assessment activities, and student performance\nmonitoring due to the increasing use of digital learning platforms in education.\nHowever, existing platf orms may not fully address the specific academic and\ninstructional needs of every educational institution. In response to these needs,\nLearnSync: A Cloud -Based AI Academic Assistant for Student Performance\nDevelopment is proposed as an integrated platform for Governor Mariano\nVillafuerte Community College (GMVCC) that supports the management of\nlearning materials, quizzes and activities, automated and manual score recording,\nstudent progress monitoring, and academic assistance based on teacher -\nuploaded learning materials.\nUniversity students often face challenges in balancing academic\nresponsibilities with personal life, which can negatively affect their performance\nand focus. Issues such as heavy course loads, low academic standing, and lack\nof awareness of schedules or ex aminations contribute to difficulties in managing\ntime and maintaining academic success.  Hajamohideen et. al (2026). The\nintegration of artificial intelligence (AI) into educational environments has opened\nnew pathways for enhancing student learning and academic support. The cloud -\nbased storage infrastructure allows students to access study resources anyti me\nand anywhere, fostering a connected and inclusive learning environment. By\nembedding advanced AI technologies into the academic ecosystem, Smart Assist\noffers 24/7 support, helping students remain organized, manage academic
172df12f-b158-42a4-a7c3-9f8ec1b35bba	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	2	Lesson 1	Page 3	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	2\nresponsibilities effectively, and ultimately improve their overall learning outcomes.\nSwetha et. al (2025).\nThis study will be conducted at Governor Mariano E. Villafuerte Community\nCollege (GMVCC), located in Potot, Libmanan, Camarines Sur. GMVCC is a public\ninstitution dedicated to providing free quality education, giving students the\nopportunity to pursue college degrees without tuition or other fees. For the Second\nSemester of Academic Year 2025 –2026, the college recorded a total enrollment\nof 1,789 students across its programs. Specifically, the Bachelor of Technical -\nVocational Teacher Education in Automotiv e Technology (BTVTED -AUTO) has\n294 students, Electrical Technology (BTVTED -ELC) has 249 students, and\nElectronics Technology (BTVTED -ELX) has 336 students. The Bachelor of\nScience in Entrepreneurship (BS ENTREP) accounts for the largest group with 801\nstudents, while the Bachelor of Science in Information Systems (BSIS) has 61\nstudents, and the Associate in Computer Technology (ACT) program has 48\nstudents. The primary client for this research is Ma’am Maricel R. Peneo, the\nschool administrator of GMVCC, wh ose involvement ensures that the project\naligns with the academic objectives of the institution and addresses the needs of\nboth faculty and students. By situating the study within GMVCC, the research gains\ncontextual relevance and contributes to the instit ution’s mission of fostering\naccessible, organized, and effective higher education for the local community.\nGovernor Mariano E. Villafuerte Community College (GMVCC) in Potot,\nLibmanan, Camarines Sur, despite its commitment to providing free quality\neducation, continues to face challenges in effectively integrating technology into
2e7726c3-e44c-4b2c-888a-69c417265fb7	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	3	Lesson 1	Page 4	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	3\nacademic processes. The institution currently lacks a centralized system that can\nprovide organized academic support and guidance to students. As a result,\nlearners often struggle to access resources efficiently, leading to delays in\nretrieving important m aterials and difficulties in managing their study\nresponsibilities. Instructional delivery is further hindered by the absence of a\nstructured Learning Management System (LMS), which results in inconsistencies\nin course content distribution and limited oppo rtunities for interactive learning.\nTeachers are burdened with manual recording and grading of assessments, a\nprocess that consumes significant time and effort while increasing the risk of\nerrors. Additionally, the lack of a comprehensive data management s ystem\nprevents the institution from systematically tracking and analyzing student\nperformance. This gap delays timely interventions for struggling students and\ncontributes to widening academic imbalances.\nThe researchers address the academic and technological challenges of\nGovernor Mariano E. Villafuerte Community College (GMVCC) by introducing an\nintegrated system equipped with a Learning Management System (LMS), a\nrecording and grading sheet, and a data m anagement framework. The proposed\nsystem will operate within the institution to provide organized academic support\nand streamline instructional delivery. The LMS will serve as a centralized platform\nwhere teachers can upload lesson modules, quizzes, and ac tivities, ensuring\nconsistent and accessible course content delivery. To reduce teacher workload\nand minimize errors, a recording and grading sheet will be developed to\naccommodate both automated and manual input assessments. Automated
3a45dcbc-6a9e-4c0f-8adb-92d5e3ea0227	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	42	Lesson 1	Page 43	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	42\nprovides the appropriate records, results, responses, and academic information to\neach user. This supports the main functions of LearnSync, including user\nmanagement, learning material management, assessment management, AI -\nassisted academic support, grade management, and student performance\nmonitoring.\nFigure 3.5\nData Flow Diagram for Admin\nFigure 3.5 illustrates the Data Flow Diagram (DFD) of the Admin Module of\nLEARNSYNC: A Cloud -Based AI Academic Assistant for Student Performance\nDevelopment. It shows how the administrator manages user accounts, validates\naccount information, imports user records, and views or updates existing user\nrecords. The diagram also shows how account data is stored and retrieved from\nthe Account (D1) data store.\nThe process starts with Create User Account (1.1), where the Admin\nprovides the necessary user account details. The account details are then passed\nto Validate and Save User Data (1.3), where the information is checked and saved.
e85ecaf1-c7a5-4121-8bea-cbbd6b314af0	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	4	Lesson 1	Page 5	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	4\nrecording will cover online minor assessments such as quizzes and the scoring of\nasynchronous activities submitted in PDF format, while manual recording will\ninclude class participation, attendance, reporting, and recitations. Since grading\nsystems vary ac ross subjects, the sheet will also feature customizable grade\npercentages, allowing teachers to adjust weightings according to their specific\nrequirements. Complementing these features, a data management system will be\nestablished to systematically track a nd analyze student outcomes, providing\nactionable insights for academic decision -making and performance monitoring.\nCollectively, these solutions aim to enhance student learning, reduce teacher\nworkload, and promote efficiency in instructional delivery, thereby fostering a more\neffective and sustainable academic environment at GMVCC. The AI academic\nassistant of LearnSync will be powered by the Llama 3.3 70B model, an instruction-\ntuned large language model developed by Meta. The model is designed to\nunderstand user questions and generate relevant, clear, and contextual\nresponses. In the proposed system, it will support students by providing academic\nassistance, guided explanations, question answering, and summaries based on\nrelevant learning materials provided through LearnSync. The use of Llama 3.3 70B\nprovides the system with a capable language model for handling complex\nacademic queries and generating natural-language responses, supporting the goal\nof LearnSync to improve students’ access to academic assis tance and learning\nsupport.
05b13986-46dc-41cb-8c79-4c37c97b4c91	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	5	Lesson 1	Page 6	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	5\nGeneral Objectives\nTo develop and implement a comprehensive Learning Management System (LMS)\nthat supports teaching, learning, and assessment processes.\nSpecific Objectives\n1. To develop an Assistant for academic guidance support for students and\nassessment generator for Teachers.\n2. To design LMS for delivering assessment and course content.\n3. To implement automated and manual assessment recording sheet for minor\nand major assessments.\n4. To track the student’s learning progress based on instructor’s sylabus.
2f24f5c5-fde0-462d-b950-8275ca8d19fb	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	6	Lesson 1	Page 7	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	6\nFigure 1\nLEARNSYNC: A Cloud-Based AI Academic Assistant for Student\nPerformance Development\nThe Input of LearnSync consists of the teacher -uploaded modules, quizzes and\nactivities, student and teacher accounts, student submissions, quiz and activity\nscores, cloud infrastructure, and the Llama 3.3 70B Versatile AI model. These\ninputs provide the i nstructional content, user information, assessment data, and\ntechnical resources required for the operation of the system. The uploaded\nlearning materials serve as the primary source of academic content, while student
e5b6bf84-502b-4993-8c32-86005ac63c1b	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	7	Lesson 1	Page 8	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	7\nsubmissions and assessment scores provide information that can be used to\nmonitor and evaluate student performance. The Llama 3.3 70B Versatile model\nsupports the AI Academic Assistant in understanding questions and generating\nacademic responses based on the relevant information provided by LearnSync.\nThe Process consists of the Learning Management System and the AI Academic\nAssistant. The LMS allows teachers to upload and manage learning materials,\ncreate quizzes and activities, record scores, and manage student learning\nresources. Students can access the available materials, answer quizzes, and\nsubmit their activities through the platform. The system records assessment results\nand student activities to support progress monitoring. Meanwhile, the AI Academic\nAssistant processes relevant teacher-provided learning content to assist students\nwith academic questions, provide guided explanations, generate summaries, and\nretrieve relevant content. Through these processes, LearnSync integrates learning\nmanagement and AI-assisted academic support into one platform.\nThe output of the system  includes centralized access to learning materials,\nautomated quiz scoring and manual score recording, student progress monitoring,\nAI-assisted learning support, and performance reports and analytics. These\noutputs allow GMVCC students to access their learn ing resources and receive\nacademic assistance through the platform, while teachers can manage\ninstructional materials, record assessment results, and monitor student progress.\nThe performance reports and analytics can also provide teachers and\nadministrators with organized information about student performance, which can
ea587fd6-59b7-4e5a-80d4-46c0a6b742f8	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	8	Lesson 1	Page 9	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	8\nsupport the identification of learning needs and improve the management of\nlearning activities.\nScope and Delimitation\nAssistant for Student Performance Development for the Bachelor of\nScience in Entrepreneurship program at Governor Mariano E. Villafuerte\nCommunity College (GMVCC), Libmanan, Camarines Sur. The system is designed\nto provide an integrated platform for managi ng learning materials, classes,\nquizzes, activities, and student academic records. The admin is responsible for\nmanaging user accounts, monitoring system activities, and accessing feedback\nand reports. Teachers can upload learning materials, create and manage quizzes\nand activities, record assessment scores, and monitor student performance.\nStudents can access their enrolled classes, view learning materials, take online\nquizzes, submit activities, and use the AI Academic Assistant for academic support\nbased on the learning materials provided by their teachers. The system also\nincludes an AI -assisted quiz generation feature that can create draft questions\nfrom uploaded learning materials, which teachers can review before use. The\nstudy also covers the monitor ing and recording of student performance through\nquiz results, activity scores, and other assessment records. The system is intended\nto support administrators and teachers in managing academic activities and help\nstudents access organized learning resource s and academic assistance through\na cloud-based platform.
24c63b5f-a2d0-4905-b89f-27e55d091bb0	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	9	Lesson 1	Page 10	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	9\nThis study is limited to the development and implementation of LearnSync:\nA Cloud-Based AI Academic Assistant for Student Performance Development for\nthe Bachelor of Science in Entrepreneurship program at Governor Mariano E.\nVillafuerte Community College (GMVCC), Libmanan, Camarines Sur. The system\nis intended only for authorized administrators, teachers, and students within the\nselected program and does not cover other academic programs or institutions. The\nsystem focuses on managing learning materials, classes, quizzes, activities, user\naccounts, academic records, and student performance. Automated assessment\nchecking is limited to online quizzes and does not cover asynchronous activities or\nother major assessments that require teacher evaluation. Activiti es, face-to-face\nassessments, participation, attendance, and other assessments that require\nteacher judgment are recorded or graded manually. Teachers are required to\nreview AI -generated quizzes before they are delivered to students. The AI\nAcademic Assistant is limited to generating responses based on the modules and\nlearning materials uploaded by teachers to ensure alignment with the approved\ninstructional content. The study does not include advanced predictive analytics,\nautomated prediction of student a cademic performance, or integration with\nexternal learning management systems and other educational platforms. The\nsystem also does not replace the role of teachers or administrators in evaluating\nstudents, managing classes, and making academic decisions.
e8d652f0-2fbd-4025-9fbb-519bca011cc3	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	10	Lesson 1	Page 11	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	10\nSignificant of the Study\nThe LearnSync system is expected to generate meaningful benefits to the\nfollowing:\nTeachers. Saves time and resources by centralizing module uploads, quiz\ncreation, and student performance monitoring in one cloud-based platform.\nStudents. Ensures consistent access to lessons and activities, with AI\nassistance to support comprehension using teacher-provided materials.\nResearcher. Enhances technical expertise and contributes new knowledge\nto educational technology.\nFuture Researchers. Serves as a reference for integrating cloud -based\nsystems and AI in education, guiding future improvements and applications.\nDefinition of Terms\nLearning Management System (LMS). A centralized digital platform used\nto deliver, manage, and monitor academic content such as modules, quizzes, and\nactivities. In this study, the LMS serves as the backbone of instructional delivery\nfor GMVCC’s BS Entrepreneurship program, ensuring consist ent access to\nlearning materials and assessments.\nArtificial Intelligence (AI) Assistant . Known as a artificial intelligence\nassistant is a digital tool or application that uses generative AI technologies to\nperform tasks, answer questions, or execute commands. In this study, the AI
26638095-68ac-4cba-99c2-0eddf42090cc	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	11	Lesson 1	Page 12	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	11\nassistant answers student queries and aids comprehension strictly and generates\nquizzes based on teacher-uploaded materials, ensuring curriculum alignment.\nCloud-Based Platform. Known as cloud computing, is a broad term for\nanything that involves the delivery of hosted services via the Internet.  It supports\nflexibility, inclusivity, and efficiency in academic processes.\nRecording and Grading Sheet . A digital tool that accommodates both\nautomated and manual input of student performance. Automated features cover\nquizzes and minor assessments, while manual recording includes participation,\nattendance, reporting, and recitations. It also allows customiza ble grade\npercentages to fit subject-specific requirements.\nData Management Framework. A structured system for tracking, storing,\nand analyzing data. In this study, it provides instructors with actionable insights\ninto student progress, enabling timely interventions and informed academic\ndecision-making.\nProgress Tracking. Monitoring progress toward a specific goal over time.\nIn this study, it is used to track lesson progress based on class syllabus.
837bc156-5725-4646-a2d9-ac4619745b34	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	12	Lesson 1	Page 13	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	12\nNOTES\nHajamohideen, F., Subramanian, K., Vimbi, V., Shafee, N., Al Mamari, R. Z., Al\nMamari, M. A., & Al Balushi, N. M. (2026). AI -based digital academic\nassistance for supporting university students. In Innovations in\nmanagement, technology, and performance  (pp. 225 –240). CRC Press.\nhttps://doi.org/10.1201/9781003778905-18\nSwetha, C., Ismail, M., Anil, J., Venkatesh, K., & Rasheed, S. (2025). AI powered\nacademic assistant using conversational AI. 2025 International Conference\non Information Technology Trends\nhttps://doi.org/10.1109/ITT58520.2025.11012528\nHealey, M. (2025). What is a learning management system (LMS)? Examples,\ntypes & pros/cons . Discovery Education.\nhttps://www.discoveryeducation.com/blog/educational-leadership/learning-\nmanagement-system/\nKantor, A. (2026, January 15). AI assistant explained: Complete guide for 2026 .\nThe Business Dive. https://thebusinessdive.com/ai-assistant-explained\nUnity Communications. (2025, May 26). What is a cloud -based system and how\ndoes it work?  Unity Communications. https://unity-connect.com/our-\nresources/tech-insights/what-is-a-cloud-based-system-and-how-does-it-\nwork/\nFirican, G. (2025). Data management framework 101 . LightsOnData.\nhttps://www.lightsondata.com/data-management-framework/\nAlesforce. (n.d.). What is an AI assistant?\nhttps://www.salesforce.com/ap/agentforce/ai-assistant/ Salesforce.\nAnderson C. (2024, September 6). What is progress tracking systems?\nhttps://focuskeeper.co/glossary/what-is-progress-tracking-systems
aea315e5-309a-4660-a344-2b316030a9d9	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	13	Lesson 1	Page 14	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	13\nCHAPTER II\nREVIEW OF RELATED LITERATURE AND STUDIES\nThis chapter presents the review of related literature and studies collected\nfrom a range of credible references. It provides an organized discussion of existing\nworks that are directly relevant to the topic, highlighting their contributions and\ninsights. The review is arranged according to specific areas of concern, ensuring\nthat each source is examined in relation to its relevance to the study.\nAI Assistant\nArtificial Intelligence (AI) and Knowledge Management (KM) are\ninterconnected, with AI enabling machine learning and KM supporting knowledge\nsharing. A systematic review using PRISMA and PICO found that communication,\ntrust, information systems, incentives , and organizational structure influence\nknowledge sharing. Integrating AI into KM enhances knowledge cycles, enterprise\nperformance, and distribution, with leadership and organizational arrangements\nensuring stability. The study, the first to examine KM w ith AI, calls for future\nresearch on smart assistant systems to strengthen competitiveness and deliver\nsocial benefits. Pai et.al (2021). Swetha et al. (2025) introduced Smart Assist, an\nAI-based academic assistant using NLP, machine learning, and cloud co mputing\nto provide secure, personalized student support with features like authenticated\naccess, interactive chat, cloud storage, and 24/7 assistance. Built -in analytics\nenhance performance and organization, while AI integration has transformed\nLearning Ma nagement Systems (LMSs) through personalization and distance\nlearning. A PRISMA -2020 bibliometric analysis shows rapid growth in AI -LMS
d56e63ea-94ab-4118-8780-2af51c989a7a	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	14	Lesson 1	Page 15	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	14\nresearch, led by authors such as Li J and Cavus N, with strong contributions from\nChina and India. Despite advances in e -learning and emerging themes like\nlearning styles and ontology, gaps remain in the effectiveness and acceptance of\nvirtual assistants, highlighting the need for future research.\nLearning Assistant Management and Builder (LAMB) is an open -source\nframework that integrates AI assistants into Learning Management Systems (LMS)\nwhile ensuring privacy, policy alignment, and reliable information use. Its modular\ndesign and features such as prompt engineering, retrieval-augmented generation,\nand knowledge base creation allow educators to build custom assistants without\ncoding. Validated through the “Macroeconomics Study Coach,” LAMB has shown\npotential to enhance teaching, increase student engagement, and deliver\npersonalized learning experiences. Its scalability, security, and open -source\nnature further promote collaboration and innovation in AI -driven education\n(Forment et al., 2025).  Raghavendrachar et.al (2023) Developed  AI -powered\nLearning Management System (LMS) for web development and coding, integrating\na chatbot like ChatGPT to enhance learning efficiency. The LMS features\ndocument and video modules, step-by-step project guides, career roadmaps, and\nresources for problem solving an d interview preparation. Its main goal is to\nevaluate how AI assistants accelerate skill acquisition, support app development,\nand improve learners’ employability. The paper assesses the effectiveness of each\nfeature, offering a holistic view of how techno logy can streamline learning and\ncareer readiness.
8b569967-49e9-4efa-9cfd-f5598b370630	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	15	Lesson 1	Page 16	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	15\nThe advent of Artificial Intelligence (AI) has transformed Learning\nManagement Systems (LMSs), enabling personalization and distance education.\nUsing PRISMA -2020, this study applies bibliometric analysis to examine AI\nintegration in LMSs, revealing rapid g rowth with a 99.42% increase, especially in\n2015 and 2021. Influential contributions include authors Li J and Cavus N, the\njournal Lecture Notes in Computer Science , and countries such as China and\nIndia. Thematic clusters highlight e-learning, ontology, and ant colony optimization,\nwhile emerging terms like Learning Style and LMS suggest areas for further study.\nDespite progress, gaps remain in the effectiveness and  acceptance of virtual\nassistants, underscoring the need for a future research agenda. Celerina et.al\n(2025).\nLearning Management System\nThe relationship between Learning Management System (LMS) usage and\nacademic performance among 188 Filipino senior high school students. In today’s\ntechnology-driven education, institutions adopt ICTs, LMS platforms, and blended\nlearning to improve student  outcomes. However, findings based on the Pearson\ncorrelation coefficient revealed no significant relationship between LMS usage and\nacademic performance. This highlights that while LMSs provide access and\nsupport, their effectiveness in directly influenci ng student achievement remains\nuncertain, pointing to the need for further investigation into how LMS integration\nimpacts learning results. Tus et.al (2021). Technology has transformed education\nby moving beyond traditional classroom settings into intercon nected, flexible\nlearning environments. Modern learners increasingly reject rigid, stand-and-deliver
87add296-ced3-4fc7-95e4-def823f463ed	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	16	Lesson 1	Page 17	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	16\napproaches tied to fixed times and locations, favoring accessible, mobile, and\npersonalized modes of participation. Advances in communication technologies\nand mobile devices have enabled learners to access knowledge remotely, while\nsoftware vendors, open -source developers, and educational institutions have\nembraced systems that manage courses and engage students online. This shift\nhighlights the growing importance of digital platforms in meeting the evolving\ndemands of global education. Turnbul et.al(2020).  The study of Rabiman et.al\n(2020) highlights the weaknesses of traditional learning, where educators and\nstudents face limitations in repeating material, note -taking, and access to\nknowledge restricted by time and space. With students increasingly dependent on\ndigital content, online learning offers positive opportunities for accessible, storable,\nand shareable teaching materials. Using the Hannafin and Peck model (needs\nanalysis, design, development, and implementation), researchers developed and\ntested an LMS-based e-learning system in a Mechanical Engineering Education\nmicroteaching class. Validated by experts and tested with 15 undergraduate\nstudents, the LMS was rated “very feasible” based on usability, functions,\ncommunication, design, and content. Fi ndings show that LMS use improves\nsatisfaction and learning quality, demonstrating its effectiveness in modern\neducation.\nThe study of Obana (2021) Learning Management System: An Essential\nTool to Enhance Remote Education. The Learning Management Systems (LMS)\nare essential in education and corporate learning because they provide flexible,\naccessible, and organized platforms for delivering online and blended learning. For
60437a90-1a8e-42b7-921a-58f9217ad5a8	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	17	Lesson 1	Page 18	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	17\nstudents, LMS enables anytime, anywhere learning, allowing them to access\ncourses, track progress, manage assignments, and interact with professors\nworldwide. Teachers benefit by uploading reusable materials, creating tests, giving\nreal-time feedback, and monitoring student performance through analytics. A\nunique feature, multi-tenancy, allows schools to share resources while maintaining\nindividual branding. In corporate settings, LMS supports employee development\nby tracking competencies and ensuring compl iance, especially in regulated\nindustries. Overall, LMS enhances efficiency, accessibility, and collaboration,\nmaking it a vital tool for modern education and workforce training. Rosario and\nDias (2022) Indicates That the Learning Management System (LMS) is a vital tool\nin education as it manages, tracks, and delivers learning materials while organizing\nstudent records. Its adoption has revolutionized education by offering a\ncomprehensive view of teaching, learning, and performance processes across\nstudents, teachers, and administrators. A systematic bibliometric literature review\n(LRSB) of 49 SCOPUS-indexed documents revealed the growth and innovations\nin LMS, identifying both opportunities and challenges in implementation. Findings\nemphasize that LMS desig ners and developers must understand the needs of\ndifferent user groups to ensure tools and resources align with their expectations,\nultimately improving student outcomes and learning quality.\nPerformance Monitoring\nEducational Data Mining for Monitoring and Improving Academic\nPerformance at University Levels , Educational Data Mining on 712 Moodle LMS\nlogs from an African University to analyze student and staff usage patterns and
64c55505-01dc-4578-b19c-f461d97ebeb9	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	18	Lesson 1	Page 19	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	18\ntheir impact on academic performance. Data was processed using machine\nlearning algorithms (clustering, classification, visualization) in WEKA, while SPSS\ncorrelation analysis examined links between course tool usage (Quiz, Assignment,\nForum, Chat, Files, etc.) and final grades. Results showed significant correlations\nat the 0.01 level, indicating that higher engagement with LMS resources positively\ninfluenced student performance. Findings highlight the strategic value of LMS data\nfor academic planning and improving learning outcomes. Okike and Mogorosi\n(2020). Costa et.al (2020) developed Monitoring Academic Performance Based on\nLearning Analytics and Ontology: A Systematic Review\nthe coordinated use of Learning Analytics and Computational Ontologies in\nevaluating student performance. From 1,230 studies, 31 were analyzed focusing\non their use in describing Taxonomies of Educational Objectives and applying\nanalytical models to educat ional data. Results show few studies connect LMS\ninteractions to learning experiences, most use ontologies for learning object\nassessment and sequencing, and none report coordinated use for performance\nmonitoring. The review concludes that future research should develop new models\nintegrating both approaches to improve academic performance evaluation.\nThe study of academic performance monitoring system using least squares\nregression in MATLAB to predict student outcomes. A GUI was designed to\nforecast relationships between factors, with accuracy measured through\nPercentage Forecast Error (PFE) and Mean Absolute Percent Error (MAPE). The\nsample included 182 BS Information Technology students, with 13 selected via\nrandom sampling and their GPAs used for prediction. Results show the system
b19811f6-1887-47c0-aa69-f01204a1d63d	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	19	Lesson 1	Page 20	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	19\neffectively monitors and forecasts performance, supporting improved learning\noutcomes and academic planning. Pilarca(2023). The study developed of  a web-\nbased centralized tracking system for academic performance to address issues in\ntraditional methods such as fragmented data, manual errors, and lack of real-time\naccess. The system provides a unified platform for storing and managing student\nrecords, assessments, and progress report s, ensuring accessibility, scalability,\nand secure data handling. Findings highlight its role in enhancing transparency,\nidentifying learning gaps, supporting outcome -based education, and generating\nanalytical reports. Overall, web -based tracking systems m odernize academic\nmanagement and contribute to continuous quality improvement in higher\neducation. Karper et.al (2025).\nThe limitations of Learning Management Systems (LMSs) in monitoring\nstudent progress and proposes the use of Learning Analytics with Student\nPerformance Indicators (SPIs). Conducted at Cadi Ayyad University in Morocco,\nthe case study tracked students in a Web Technologies course using SPIs to\nanalyze instructional progress. Findings show that SPIs help faculty identify\nunderperforming students, anticipate those at risk, and provide timely\ninterventions. The research highlights that transforming LMS data into meaningful\nindicators enhances learner self -regulation, engagement, and academic support.\nQazdar et.al (2023).\nAutomated & Manual Recording
ba470554-3bd4-4387-96c1-30371bb60a57	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	20	Lesson 1	Page 21	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	20\nAutomated recording systems improve the efficiency and quality of\ndocumentation compared to manual recording methods. Their study explained that\nautomated systems reduce the workload of employees because data are recorded\nautomatically and accurately. On the other hand, manual recording requires more\ntime and effort since information is written by hand. The researchers stated that\nautomated systems help lessen documentation errors and improve record\nmanagement. Tse et al. (2020).\nThe study of Minor et al. (2021) found that automated monitoring systems\nprovide more accurate and consistent records than manual recording systems.\nThe researchers explained that manual recording is more likely to contain\nincomplete or delayed information because it depends on human observation and\nmanual input. The study concluded that automated systems improve the reliability\nof recorded data and support better monitoring processes.\nThe study conducted by Paulsen (2020) discussed the differences between\nmanual data entry and automated form-processing technologies. The researchers\nexplained that manual recording is reliable but requires more labor, time, and cost.\nAutomated technologies such as Optical Mark Recognition (O MR) and Intelligent\nCharacter Recognition (ICR) improved efficiency and reduced the processing time\nof records. The study highlighted the importance of automation in managing large\namounts of data effectively.\nAccording to Henning et al. (2020) , digital or automated record systems\nimprove communication and make documentation faster during emergency
ef647552-a22a-4ac6-a7d9-045d76e46eff	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	21	Lesson 1	Page 22	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	21\nsituations. Their study found that manual recording methods were slower and more\nlikely to miss important information during critical operations. The researchers\nconcluded that automated systems provide better support in managing records\nduring emergencies.\nThe study of Bansal and Sharma (2025)  stated that automated school\nrecord management systems improve the speed and accuracy of processing\nstudent information compared to manual systems. The researchers found that\nautomated systems were easier to use and more effective in organizing records.\nThey recommended the use of automated systems in schools to improve record\nmanagement and operational efficiency.\nProgress Tracking\nShen et al. (2022) proposed a novel paradigm in Knowledge Tracing (KT)\nthrough the Learning Process-consistent Knowledge Tracing (LPKT) model, which\nemphasizes aligning students’ evolving knowledge states with their actual learning\nprocess rather than focu sing solely on single interaction outcomes. LPKT\nintegrates the positive effects of learning gains and the negative effects of\nforgetting to capture student progress across continuous learning interactions,\nthereby offering a more realistic representation of knowledge evolution. To account\nfor individual differences, they extended the model to LPKT -S, which explicitly\ndistinguishes each student’s progress rate. Experimental results on three public\ndatasets showed that both LPKT and LPKT-S not only outperform state-of-the-art\nKT methods in predicting student performance but also provide knowledge states
aa0dccd4-a4b4-4740-a8e7-eca20ee4dce2	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	49	Lesson 1	Page 50	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	49\nconnects learning modules to specific sections. This structure allows the\nresearchers to organize the instructional materials that will be used within the\nLearnSync platform.\nLearning activities and assessments are represented by the activity,\nactivity_sections, act_submission, quiz, question, quiz_sections, student_answer,\nand quiz_score entities. The researchers designed the activity entity to store\nassignments and tasks, whi le act_submission records student submissions,\nscores, feedback, and submission status. The quiz entity stores assessment\ninformation and is connected to the question entity, which contains questions,\nchoices, correct answers, points, and explanations. Stu dent responses are\nrecorded through the student_answer entity, while quiz results are stored in\nquiz_score.\nTo support student performance monitoring, the researchers included the\nattendance, act_recitation, and grade entities. Attendance records student\npresence and related scores, while act_recitation stores recitation results. The\ngrade entity stores student  performance information, including scores and totals.\nThese entities allow the researchers to organize different assessment records and\nprovide the necessary data for monitoring student performance.\nThe researchers also included the announcement and\nannouncement_section entities to support academic communication. The\nannouncement entity stores information such as the title, message, publication\ndate, and status, while announcement_section associates a nnouncements with
4049abc5-6a08-416a-a25d-c977a55dcc22	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	22	Lesson 1	Page 23	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	22\nthat are more consistent with the learning process, making them highly\ninterpretable and accurate. This work highlights a promising direction for KT\nresearch by shifting focus toward continuous, process -consistent monitoring of\nstudent learning.\nStarford and Ravlikj (2024) examined the efficacy of academic support\ninterventions provided by the Swinburne College Student Hub for international\nstudents enrolled in the Postgraduate Qualifying Program (PQP) and English\nLanguage Intensive Courses for Ov erseas Students (ELICOS). Their study\nhighlighted program-specific interventions, with PQP students receiving plagiarism\nchecking, paraphrasing, proofreading, referencing, and essay structure guidance,\nwhile ELICOS students engaged in skill -based workshops  targeting reading,\nlistening, and writing. To refine these interventions, the researchers developed\nstudent progress trackers, which revealed significant improvements in assignment\nsuccess rates and unit completion based on empirical data from 2022. Findi ngs\nunderscore the effectiveness of tailored academic support in enhancing student\noutcomes, with implications for improving the delivery of support services and\nstrengthening academic performance among PQP and ELICOS cohorts.\nAccording to Qazdar et al. (2022) they investigated the role of Learning\nManagement Systems (LMS) in supporting student progress tracking during the\nCOVID-19 pandemic, when digital learning platforms became central to higher\neducation. While LMS platforms provide essential tools for managing courses and\nadministration, the study noted their limited capacity to monitor instructional\nprogress. To address this, the researchers applied learning analytics to define
3c545e05-69f0-4c35-a103-f80bf92a01b0	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	23	Lesson 1	Page 24	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	23\nindicators capable of tracking students’ interactions with materials, identifying\nstruggling learners, and highlighting those needing intervention. Using data from a\nWeb Technologies course at Cadi Ayyad University, they developed student Key\nPerformance Indicators (KPIs) that allowed monitoring of progress throughout the\ncourse. Although these indicators effectively tracked engagement and progress,\nthey were less successful in determining overall student performance. The study\nunderscores the importance of integrating analytics into LMS to enhance student\nsuccess rates and improve the effectiveness of e-learning processes.\nYousufi et al. (2023) explored the application of Big Data Analytics (BDA) in\neducation to enhance institutional decision-making and student progress tracking.\nTheir study emphasized that analyzing large volumes of learner data enables\ninstitutions to iden tify success rates, achievements, weaknesses, and compare\nperformance against benchmarked institutions for continuous improvement.\nBenchmarking was highlighted as a valuable tool for evaluating learners, faculty,\nand curriculum outcomes relative to similar programs. The research demonstrated\nthat BDA can help decision makers detect, analyze, and predict educational\nprogress, learner behavior, and course outcomes. Ultimately, the authors\nrecommended effective implementation of BDA practices to monitor studen t\nperformance, identify areas for improvement, and strengthen employability skills,\nunderscoring its role in advancing academic success and institutional growth.\nThe study of Allam et al. (2023) investigated the use of machine learning\nalgorithms to address challenges educators face in monitoring student academic\nprogress and identifying at -risk learners. Their study emphasized that while
7c71289c-86c6-41c4-bd0d-cbcc914c092e	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	24	Lesson 1	Page 25	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	24\ninstitutions collect vast amounts of student data, leveraging this information\nthrough predictive models can enhance instructional quality and reputation. The\nproposed approach employed various machine learning techniques to create a\nmodel capable of detec ting students at risk of failing, presenting results in\naccessible visual formats that allow teachers to implement timely interventions. By\npredicting learner behavior and course outcomes early, the model supports\nproactive support strategies and lays the groundwork for future predictive systems\naimed at optimizing the overall learning process in higher education.\nSynthesis of Related Literature and Studies\nThe reviewed literature collectively shows that the integration of Artificial\nIntelligence (AI), Learning Management Systems (LMS), knowledge management,\nand learning analytics is reshaping modern education by improving knowledge\nsharing, instructional delivery, and academic performance monitoring.\nSeveral studies discuss the role of Artificial Intelligence in knowledge\nmanagement and educational systems. Studies show that Artificial Intelligence\nenhances knowledge management and educational systems by improving\nknowledge sharing, communication, and organizational performance (Pai et al.,\n2021). AI-based tools like Smart Assist provide personalized learning through NLP,\nmachine learning, secure access, and 24/7 support (Swetha et al., 2025).\nFrameworks such as LAMB enable the integration of AI assista nts into LMS\nplatforms with privacy and reliability (Forment et al., 2025). Additionally, AI -\npowered LMS platforms support coding education through chatbots, project
39ac4a62-71a2-43f7-84b3-c322d8858e3a	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	25	Lesson 1	Page 26	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	25\nguides, and career resources, improving student skills and employability\n(Raghavendrachar et al., 2023).\nResearch highlights that Learning Management Systems enable flexible,\naccessible, and organized digital learning environments (Turnbull et al., 2020).\nLMS platforms help manage learning materials, track student progress, and\nimprove communication (Rosario and Dias, 2022). They also enhance usability,\nsatisfaction, and learning quality (Rabiman et al., 2020). However, LMS usage\ndoes not always significantly impact academic performance, indicating the need\nfor further improvement (Tus et al., 2021).\nLiterature shows that academic performance monitoring systems use data\nanalytics and predictive models to track student progress and improve outcomes\n(Okike and Mogorosi, 2020). Learning analytics and ontologies support better\nevaluation of learning proces ses (Costa et al., 2020). Systems using regression\nmodels help predict performance and support planning (Pilarca, 2023), while\ncentralized tracking systems improve transparency and data management (Karper\net al., 2025). Student Performance Indicators also help identify at-risk students and\nenable early intervention (Qazdar et al., 2023).\nFor progress tracking, studies highlight the importance of continuous and\nAI-enhanced monitoring of student learning. Shen et al. (2022) introduced\nknowledge tracing models that capture learning as a continuous process,\nincorporating both learning gains an d forgetting patterns. Similarly, Starford and\nRavlikj (2024) and Yousufi et al. (2023) emphasized that progress tracking
5b5cd70e-5fbe-4ab5-a148-8c473ac7f852	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	26	Lesson 1	Page 27	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	26\nsystems and big data analytics improve student monitoring, institutional decision -\nmaking, and academic support strategies. These studies demonstrate that\ncombining analytics with LMS data significantly enhances educational outcomes.\nThe literature on automated and manual recording consistently shows the\nadvantages of automation in education systems. Automated systems improve\naccuracy, reduce human error, and enhance efficiency compared to manual\nrecording methods, which are often time-consuming and prone to inconsistencies\n(Tse et al., 2020; Paulsen, 2020; Bansal & Sharma, 2025). These findings support\nthe transition toward digital record management systems in academic institutions.\nNOTES\nPai, R. Y., Shetty, A., Shetty, A. D., Bhandary, R., Shetty, J., Nayak, S., Dinesh, T.\nK., & D'souza, K. J. (2021). Integrating artificial intelligence for knowledge\nmanagement systems synergy among people and technology: A systematic\nreview of the evidenc e. Economic Research -Ekonomska Istraživanja.\nhttps://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/1331677X.2022.2058976\nSwetha, C., Ismail, M., Anil, J., Venkatesh, K., & Rasheed, S. (2025). AI powered\nacademic assistant using conversational AI. International Conference on\nAdvancements in Electrical, Electronics, Communication, Computing and\nAutomation. https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11012528\nAlier Forment, M., Pereira, J., García-Peñalvo, F. J., & Casañ, M. J. (2025). LAMB:\nAn open -source software framework to create artificial intelligence\nassistants deployed and integrated into learning management systems.\nComputer Standards & Interfaces, 92 (4), 103940.\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/386451753\nRaghavendrachar, S., Anand, V. S., Anushree, H., & Manjunath, R. (2023). E -\nlearning management system with AI assistance. International Journal for\nResearch in Applied Science and Engineering Technology.\nhttps://elicit.com/review/fadce8c1 8904 -4037-b4aa-\n34f331731562/source/ss-265299958
