38e5ac19-9187-43e8-a899-32e33a7a0ba8	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	31	Lesson 1	Page 32	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	31\nCHAPTER III\nMETHODOLOGY\nIn this chapter it presented the methods and procedure employed by the\nresearchers to conduct the study, aligning with the project’s objectives. It outlined\nand elaborated on the specific actions and tasks undertaken to complete the\nproject.\nResearch Design\nThe researcher uses a development and descriptive method approach in\nobtaining the system desired in this study. The researcher utilized a range of data\ncollection techniques such as interviews using Direct/Interview method, survey,\nobservations, and analy sis of existing documents. The methods used by the\nresearchers enabled to gather and acquire the essentials information and data\nnecessary of constructing the study and understanding the questions.\nResearch Methodology\nThe Agile Model is a process model within the Software Development Life\nCycle (SDLC) that emphasizes iterative development, customer collaboration, and\nflexible responses to change. Liyanage (2021). The researchers chose the\nIterative Incremental Model as the development approach for the project. This\nmodel is a smart way to design complex  systems by breaking the project into\nsmaller parts. Each part is developed, tested, and added to the system bit by bit.\nThis method is suitable for projects where requirements may change or are not\nfully clear from the start. It allows the researchers to focus on developing and
679dd892-1423-461e-ac21-6e69928502a5	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	48	Lesson 1	Page 49	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	48\nFigure 3.7 illustrates the Entity Relationship Diagram (ERD) of\nLEARNSYNC: A Cloud -Based AI Academic Assistant for Student Performance\nDevelopment. The ERD presents the organization and relationships of the\ndatabase entities designed by the researchers to support user management, class\norganization, learning materials, assessments, student submissions, grading,\nannouncements, and AI-assisted academic support.\nAt the center of the database structure is the account entity, which stores\nessential user information such as user ID, name, email, password, role, and\naccount creation date. The researchers connected this entity to the teacher and\nstudent entities to properly identify and manage the different users of LearnSync.\nTeachers are associated with classes, while students are connected to classes\nand sections through enrollment.\nThe academic structure designed by the researchers is represented by\nthe class, section, enrollment, and grading_policy entities. The class entity stores\ninformation about the class, subject, and assigned teacher, while the section entity\norganizes student s according to their assigned sections. The enrollment entity\nconnects students to their sections. The grading_policy entity stores the grading\nweights used for different assessment components.\nFor learning materials, the researchers included the module,\nmodule_content, and module_sections entities. The module entity stores\ninformation about uploaded learning materials, while module_content stores the\nextracted text and corresponding content chun ks. The module_sections entity
fe473349-0590-45f9-926d-c8ec29749bd9	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	32	Lesson 1	Page 33	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	32\nimproving each part before adding the next, helping ensure that every feature\nworks properly and contributes to a functional and organized system.\nGeeksforGeeks (2024).\nBy choosing the Iterative Incremental Model, the researchers can develop\nthe LearnSync system gradually by focusing on one feature at a time. This model\nis suitable for LearnSync, as its features can be developed, tested, and improved\nseparately. It allows  the researchers to identify errors early, make necessary\nchanges, and ensure that each feature works properly before proceeding to the\nnext stage. This helps the researchers create a reliable and functional system that\nmeets the needs of its intended users.\nFigure 3.1\nAGILE ITERATIVE INCREMENTAL MODEL
500db220-3c09-4f20-aadc-48fe3793e489	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	33	Lesson 1	Page 34	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	33\nPhase 1: Requirements Planning\nDuring the initial phase of project planning, the researcher listed the key\nrequirements and identified possible challenges. They also prepared proactive\nsolutions to handle these challenges during the development process.\nThe requirements planning phase began with consultations at Governor\nMariano Villafuerte Community College, where the researcher engaged with the\nprogram head, instructors, students, and the identified client. Through these\ndiscussions, the researcher identified the school’s pressing challenges, particularly\nin inefficiencies such as delays in retrieving materials, inconsistencies in course\ncontent distribution, and limited opportunities for interactive learning. These issues\nhindered effective teaching and  learning, consumed significant time and\nresources, and prevented timely identification of struggling students.\nTeachers emphasized the need for a centralized system that would allow\nthem to record in grading sheets, accommodating both automatically checked\nonline quizzes and manual recorded assessments, while also monitoring student\nperformance in one platform. Stu dents expressed the importance of consistent\naccess to learning materials and support mechanisms that could help them\ncomprehend lessons more effectively. Administrators highlighted insights into\ninstitutional policies and performance monitoring needs. The se consultations\nestablished the foundation for the LearnSync project, ensuring that the system’s\ndesign directly responds to the school’s needs.\nFrom these the functional requirements were defined teachers must be\nable to upload text -based materials, create quizzes, assign activities, and record\nscores, while students should access these resources and interact with an AI
42787f80-48ae-49c3-9689-55877ae559c9	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	34	Lesson 1	Page 35	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	34\nassistant that provides summaries, guided explanations, and adaptive feedback\nstrictly from teacher-uploaded content. Accuracy of AI responses, and ease of use\nfor both teachers and students.\nFigure 3.1 show as the hardware requirements to successfully\nimplemented and utilized the system. Computer hardware refers to the physical\ncomponents that make up a computer system and enable it to perform essential\nfunctions, such as input, output, proces sing and storage. Susnja and Smalley\n(2026).\nTable 3.1\nHardware Requirements for LearnSync: A Cloud-Based AI Academic\nAssistant for Student Performance Development\nHardware Requirements\nHardware Minimum Recommended\nProcessor At least Dual Core\nprocessor\nCore i3 and above, latest\ngeneration processor\nRAM 8GB 16 GB or more\nStorage 128 GB SSD 256 GB SSD or higher\nThe processor, RAM, and storage each play a distinct role in supporting\nthe system’s performance. The processor acts as the central unit that enables the\nlearning management platform and the AI academic assistant to run smoothly.\nWhile a Celeron processor can handle basic tasks such as uploading modules and\naccessing files, a Core i3 or higher is recommended to efficiently process AI\nassistant, generate summaries, and provide adaptive feedback without delays.\nRAM, on the other hand, ensures responsiveness a nd multitasking capabilities.\nWith multiple students and teachers accessing the system simultaneously through
1c6281ac-45ad-47b9-b584-677111efde78	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	35	Lesson 1	Page 36	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	35\nthe LAN, a minimum of 8 GB allows basic functionality, but 16 GB or more is\nnecessary to prevent lags and ensure real -time performance when handling\nquizzes, modules, and AI responses.\nStorage capacity is equally critical, as it houses lesson modules, quizzes,\nstudent records, and AI training data. An SSD provides faster file access compared\nto traditional HDDs, ensuring quick retrieval of instructional materials. While 128\nGB is suffici ent for initial deployment, 256 GB or higher is recommended to\naccommodate scalability, supporting larger datasets, more subjects, and future\nexpansion of the system. Altogether, these hardware requirements guarantee that\nLearnSync can function as a lightw eight, AI -powered learning management\nsystem tailored to low-resource environments. Meeting the minimum specifications\nensures usability, while the recommended specifications provide sustainability and\nscalability, aligning with the project’s goal of bridging digital divides and enhancing\nteaching and learning.\nTable 3.2\nSoftware Requirements for LearnSync: A Cloud-Based AI Academic\nAssistant for Student Performance Development\nSoftware Requirements\nSoftware Minimum Recommended\nOperating System Windows 10/Android 10  Windows 11/android\n10 and above\nInternet 2Mbps and above\nLanguage Python (FastAPI Framework), html, CSS, Alpine JS\nDatabase PostgeSQL
