8fa551e5-2d89-4f86-b421-b82af66f5ab3	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	38	Lesson 1	Page 39	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	38\nPhase 2: User Design\nThe user design phase focuses on creating a practical and user -friendly\ninterface by involving end -users in the design process. Researchers and\ndevelopers collaborate to identify user needs, workflows, and preferences,\nensuring the system is intuitive and efficient. This stage often includes prototypes,\nwireframes, and mock -ups that allow users to visualize the system and provide\nfeedback before full development. By engaging users early, potential issues can\nbe addressed, resulting in a final product that i s accessible, reliable, and aligned\nwith its intended tasks.\nFigure 3.3\nLEARNSYNC: A Cloud-Based AI Academic Assistant for Student\nPerformance Development\nFigure 3.3 illustrates the core architecture of the Learnsync: A Cloud-Based\nAI Academic Assistant for Student Performance Development.  It highlights the\nmain components involved in the interaction between the user and the system,\nincluding the devices, web system platform, server, database.
6b496811-90de-490e-a119-6e1d8cca11a1	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	39	Lesson 1	Page 40	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	39\nThe process begins when the user (student or teacher) accesses the\nsystem through various devices such as smartphones, tablets, or desktop\ncomputers. These devices act as the interface, allowing students to interact with\nlessons, quizzes. Teachers use the same interface to upload content, record\nscores and grades, and monitor student progress. The client devices connect to\nthe system via web browsers. They handle user inputs such as activities\nsubmissions, quiz answers, and AI assistant queries. These input s are packaged\ninto requests and sent to the server for processing.\nThe server acts as the central processing unit of the system. It receives\nrequests from client devices, processes the application logic checking quiz\nanswers, running AI generation, and manages communication between the user\ninterface and the database. In LearnSync, the server also hosts the AI academic\nassistant, which provides adaptive feedback, guided explanations, and\nperformance monitoring. While the database stores all persistent information,\nincluding user profiles, lesson modules, activity submissio ns, progress tracking,\nand quiz results. It communicates with the server to retrieve and update data in\nreal time, ensuring accuracy and availability across the system. For LearnSync,\nthis centralized database eliminates fragmented records and combines both online\nand offline scores in one platform, making grades and compilation of activities and\nassessments available for teacher in one place, and for the awareness of students.\nThis guide shows how the system flows user’s client devices server\ndatabase back to users. Each component plays a vital role in ensuring smooth\ninteraction, centralized resource management, and efficient learning delivery. This
0ec9d2ad-8c10-42ad-8278-23b59a6f84d6	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	40	Lesson 1	Page 41	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	40\narchitecture is adapted to a cloud -based system, directly addressing GMVCC’s\nlearning challenges while still providing modern AI-driven educational support.\nFigure 3.4\nContext Diagram of Learnsync: A Cloud-Based AI Academic Assistant for\nStudent Performance Development\nFigure 3.5 illustrates the Level 0 Data Flow Diagram of LearnSync, showing\nthe overall interaction between the system and its three main external entities: the\nAdmin, Teacher, and Students. The diagram presents the major data exchanged\nbetween the users an d the system, including user account information, learning\nmaterials, assessments, submissions, queries, results, grades, and student\nrecords. The LearnSync system serves as the central process that receives,\nprocesses, and provides the necessary information to each user.
1620b500-2ffd-44e3-8c38-794e6e306047	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	41	Lesson 1	Page 42	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	41\nThe Admin provides the system with information related to account creation,\nuser account details, and account update or import data. The system processes\nthese inputs to support the management of user accounts and records. The system\nalso provides the admin with user records and status, allowing the administrator to\nmanage and monitor user information within LearnSync.\nThe Teacher provides the system with student classes, modules or learning\nmaterials, quizzes, tasks or activities, face -to-face activities, and assessment\nscores. These data are processed by LearnSync to support the management of\ninstructional materials, assessments, and student performance. The system then\nprovides the teacher with quiz results, assessment results, student records, and\ngrade computation, allowing teachers to monitor student performance and manage\ntheir academic activities.\nThe Students interact with LearnSync by sending queries, answers, and\nsubmissions to the system. Students can use the platform to access their enrolled\nclasses, answer quizzes, submit activities, and send queries to the AI Academic\nAssistant. The system pr ocesses these inputs and provides students with quiz\nresults, submission results, query results, enrollment information, and grades,\nallowing them to monitor their academic activities and results.\nOverall, the Level 0 DFD shows how LearnSync serves as the central\nplatform connecting the Admin, Teacher, and Students. It receives user account\ninformation from the admin, instructional and assessment data from teachers, and\nqueries and submissions from students. The system processes these data and
05508a90-69d9-4e06-a8fb-109f592b368f	69c54389-1873-4de2-b394-b7e23a873062	9fb31b76-250b-402b-81e5-56d1d2d21dd6	8591adf5-04e8-4de2-8f24-e4125a7f4df1	10	File	Slide 11	a6808e3b-e613-4173-a64f-f95b876492f1	IV. AC vs. DC\nThe main difference between AC and DC is the direction of current flow.
bab9bf4d-d607-4691-95a5-d14dcff5879f	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	43	Lesson 1	Page 44	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	43\nThe validated user record is then stored in the Account (D1) data store to maintain\nthe user's account information.\nThe Admin can also use Import User Account (1.2) to bring existing user\naccount information into the system. The process receives the account details from\nthe Admin and produces an import result and an imported user record. The\nimported record is then passed to Validate and Save User Data (1.3) for validation\nbefore being stored in the Account (D1) data store.\nLastly, View and Update User Record (1.4) allows the Admin to manage\nexisting user information. The Admin provides search criteria or update details,\nwhile the process retrieves existing user records from Account (D1). After the\nnecessary changes are made,  the updated user record is returned to the data\nstore. The process also provides the Admin with information about user needs or\naccount status.\nFigure 3.6\nData Flow Diagram for Teacher
49b35689-e6cc-4dd7-9038-9149833b91a3	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	44	Lesson 1	Page 45	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	44\nFigure 3.5 illustrates the Data Flow Diagram (DFD) of the Teacher Module,\nshowing how data moves within the system and how the teacher interacts with\ndifferent processes and data elements. The diagram presents the core\ninstructional and administrative functionalities of the module, beginning with class\ncreation and section assignment, followed by the uploading of modules,\nmanagement of assessments, and monitoring of student performance.\nThe process starts with Create Class (1.0), where the teacher establishes\na new class to organize students and learning materials. Once the class is created,\nthe teacher proceeds to Assign Section (2.0), grouping students into appropriate\nsections for bett er management. After sections are assigned, the teacher can\nUpload Modules (2.0), which serve as the primary learning materials (LM). Within\nthis process, the system can also Extract Text from Module (2.1), converting\nmodule content (C1) into usable formats for for retrieval of data by AI Assistant.\nThe next major function is Manage Assessment (3.0), where the teacher\noversees different evaluation tools. This includes Upload Module (3.1) for\nassessment-linked materials, Upload Activities (3.2) for student tasks (Act1), and\nthe creation of quizzes (Q1) to measure student understanding. These assessment\ncomponents ensure that students are continuously evaluated through structured\nactivities and quizzes.\nLastly, the teacher can View Performance (4.0), which consolidates student\noutcomes. This process retrieves Scores (R1) from quizzes and activities, as well\nas Submissions (R2) from student work. By analyzing these results, the teacher\ngains insights into student progress, engagement, andoverall performance.
741cf18b-bfd8-4c18-9a93-4d03f17d0663	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	45	Lesson 1	Page 46	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	45\nFigure 3.7\nData Flow Diagram for Student\nFigure 3.6 illustrates the Data Flow Diagram (DFD) of the Student Module,\nshowing how data moves within the system and how the student interacts with\ndifferent processes and data elements. The diagram presents the main functions\nof the module, including cl ass enrollment, viewing quizzes, answering\nassessments, submitting activities, interacting with the AI Assistant, and\nmonitoring academic performance.
ecd9d8d8-3473-4574-a13d-749990f02997	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	46	Lesson 1	Page 47	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	46\nThe process starts with Enroll Class (1.0), where the student provides the\nnecessary enrollment information to join a class. The enrollment information is\npassed to Submit Enrollment (1.1), where the enrollment details are processed\nand stored in A1 (Enrol lment). The student can also access View Quiz, which\nretrieves available quizzes from Q1 (Quiz) for the student to answer.\nThe next major function is Answer Quiz (3.0), where the student answers\nthe assigned quiz. The quiz answers are passed to Check Quiz (3.1), where the\nanswers are evaluated and a quiz result is produced. The resulting score is stored\nin R1 (Score). The stud ent can also Submit Activity (4.0), where completed\nactivities are submitted and stored in R2 (Submission). These submissions can be\nreviewed through Teacher Evaluation (4.1), where the teacher evaluates the\nstudent's activity and provides an activity score.\nThe student can also use the AI Assistant through Query AI (2.0). In this\nprocess, the student sends an AI query, which is passed to AI Generation (2.1).\nThe AI Generation process retrieves relevant information from the Materials (LM)\ndata store and uses t he module context to generate an AI response. This allows\nthe student to receive assistance based on the available learning materials.\nLastly, the student can access View Performance (5.0) to monitor academic\nresults. This process receives quiz scores and activity scores, as well as activity\nsubmissions, to provide information about the student's performance. The student\ncan also use View  Submission (4.2) to view submitted activities and their\ncorresponding results. Overall, the DFD shows how LearnSync allows students to
60892c97-d544-4288-9f35-c11258ff1a56	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	47	Lesson 1	Page 48	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	47\nenroll in classes, access learning materials and assessments, submit activities,\nreceive AI-assisted support, and monitor their academic performance.\nFigure 3.8\nEntity Relationship Diagram of “LEARNSYNC: A Cloud-Based AI Academic\nAssistant for Student Performance Development”
bbaf0578-329c-45f5-b4bf-5d7781f7efa5	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	50	Lesson 1	Page 51	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	50\nspecific sections. This allows relevant announcements to be organized according\nto the students or sections concerned.\nFor the AI Academic Assistant, the researchers incorporated the ai_query\nand query_context entities. The ai_query entity stores the user's AI questions,\nresponses, and timestamps, while the query_context entity connects queries with\nrelevant module_content. This structure supports the researchers' design of an AI\nassistant that can use teacher -uploaded instructional materials as context when\nproviding academic assistance. This is consistent with the scope of the study,\nwhich states that the AI assistant generates responses based on teacher-uploaded\nmodules.\nOverall, the ERD demonstrates how the researchers structured and\ninterconnected the database entities of LearnSync to support its major functions.\nAccounts and users manage system access, classes and enrollment organize\nacademic participation, modules and activities provide learning content, quizzes\nand assessments measure student performance, grades and records support\nperformance monitoring, and AI queries and query context provide academic\nassistance. Through these relationships, the researchers established the database\nstructure needed to support teaching, assessment, academic assistance, and\nstudent performance development within LearnSync.
e6cb49eb-9b0d-42c8-9f43-851701f363d4	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	f91dabe0-17c1-41ad-aa08-46549c13043f	c27caa9c-e0c7-4819-99fc-304920e73f76	51	Lesson 1	Page 52	efeb3395-230e-49c9-9a8d-d60ab84da23f	51\nNOTES\nLiyanage, N. (2025). The Agile Model in SDLC: A Flexible Approach to Software\nDevelopment. https://medium.com/@nld.anuradha/the-agile-model-in-sdlc-\na-flexible-approach-to-software-development-664bf60ad9dc\nGeeksforGeeks. (2024). Iterative incremental model in designing system.\nhttps://www.geeksforgeeks.org/system-design/iterative-incremental-\nmodel-in-designing-system/
48b96e28-06ee-42f2-8911-b431651857a5	d0c9ab98-489b-4f87-be85-03db4b7960f5	eedb9551-c449-4a57-8961-30a2409b0151	baf90e3a-43f5-4b94-ac33-253f5ac50c6c	0	Lesson 1	Lesson	\N	The concept of power applies across several key domains:\n1. Physics and Engineering\nDefinition: Power measures how quickly energy is expended or work is performed (P = W / t, where W is work and t is time).\nUnits: Measured in watts (W), which equal one joule per second (1 W = 1 J/s).\nMechanical Power: Calculated as the product of force and velocity (P = F ⋅ v) or torque and angular speed (P = τ ⋅ ω).\nElectrical Power: Calculated as the product of voltage and current (P = V ⋅ I). [1, 2, 3, 4]\n2. Social Sciences and Politics\nDefinition: Power is the capacity or ability to influence, direct, dominate, or impact the behavior, beliefs, and actions of others—even in the face of resistance. [1, 2]\nAuthority vs. Power: Authority is power that carries formal legitimacy or institutional backing, whereas power can be exercised without official sanction. [1]\nTypes: Sociologists like Max Weber and modern frameworks categorize power into visible, hidden, and invisible dynamics, as well as coercion or persuasion. You can read more about how these concepts function via Encyclopaedia Britannica. [1, 2]\n3. Mathematics\nDefinition: Power (or exponentiation) indicates how many times a base number is multiplied by itself (e.g., \\(x^{n}\\)). [1]\nWould you like to dive deeper into physics calculations (formulas and examples) or explore political and social theories of power?
da0b6858-0b43-4ce8-b977-8537904a3060	738b1705-1279-4346-89ae-0ba597da5d75	4bbb2767-623a-46f3-920e-8669b36093b3	f5804c5d-f986-4ccf-8df1-e0cb1aaed462	0	lesson 2	Page 1	ce4121f2-b462-4725-af39-3602ffd14433	Generated by LearnSync. This is not an official institutional form.\nPage 1\nIT101 grades - Midterm\nSubject: IT101 - sample\nTerm: First Semester\nSections: None\nPeriod: Midterm\nBasis: Published grades (latest release per student)\nGrading policy: version 2; passing grade 75.0; raw scores (current)\nGenerated: 2026-10-08 10:49 (Asia/Manila) by Demo faculty 1\nStudents: 0\nRanking: Rank within all sections of this offering, based on Midterm published grades. 0 students ranked; 0 unranked. Ties use competition ranking (1, 2, 2, 4).\nRanking is descriptive: it orders the grades shown, applies no curve, and is not shown to students. Search or filters on screen never limit this file.\nRank\nStudent no.\nName\nSection\nAttendance\n(%)\nActivities\n(%)\nQuizzes (%)\nExamination\ns (%)\nGrade\nRemark\nStatus
aaca5b90-84cd-4845-bb76-17be38edd31c	69c54389-1873-4de2-b394-b7e23a873062	9fb31b76-250b-402b-81e5-56d1d2d21dd6	8591adf5-04e8-4de2-8f24-e4125a7f4df1	0	File	Slide 1	a6808e3b-e613-4173-a64f-f95b876492f1	EPA-2 WEEK 2
16b535e6-f613-48da-a7e0-91f307f3e508	69c54389-1873-4de2-b394-b7e23a873062	9fb31b76-250b-402b-81e5-56d1d2d21dd6	8591adf5-04e8-4de2-8f24-e4125a7f4df1	1	File	Slide 2	a6808e3b-e613-4173-a64f-f95b876492f1	Introduction\nAt the most basic level, every electrical system involves four fundamental concepts: current, voltage, resistance, and power. These concepts help us understand how electricity behaves and how circuits operate.\nThese four concepts are the basic vocabulary needed to understand and analyze electrical circuits. They are not only theoretical concepts; they are directly applied in real electronic and IT systems.\nElectrical Quantities: Current(I), Voltage(V), Resistance(R), and Power(P)\nThese four electrical quantities are the foundation of basic circuit analysis. They help us understand how electricity flows, what pushes the flow, what opposes it, and how much electrical energy is used.
3fbf2aa6-8c53-4724-8986-b357a6994dd8	69c54389-1873-4de2-b394-b7e23a873062	9fb31b76-250b-402b-81e5-56d1d2d21dd6	8591adf5-04e8-4de2-8f24-e4125a7f4df1	2	File	Slide 3	a6808e3b-e613-4173-a64f-f95b876492f1	Current (I)\nCurrent is the flow of electric charge through a circuit.\nCurrent tells us how much electrical flow is moving through a circuit.\nSymbol: I\nUnit: Ampere (A)\nExample:\n(Ohm's Law)\nI = V / R\nIf the voltage is 12 V and the resistance is 6 Ω:\nI = 12 V / 6 Ω\vI = 2 A
27614dbd-30a5-4383-9c35-45637fbf5e52	69c54389-1873-4de2-b394-b7e23a873062	9fb31b76-250b-402b-81e5-56d1d2d21dd6	8591adf5-04e8-4de2-8f24-e4125a7f4df1	4	File	Slide 5	a6808e3b-e613-4173-a64f-f95b876492f1	C. Resistance (R)\nResistance is the opposition to the flow of current in a circuit.\nResistance determines how much current can flow for a given voltage.\nSymbol: R\nUnit: Ohm (Ω)\nExample:\n(Ohm's Law)\nR = V / I\nIf the voltage is 12 V and the current is 2 A:\nR = 12 V / 2 A\vR = 6 Ω
53f35501-3f23-41f6-bc78-7121d5a90252	69c54389-1873-4de2-b394-b7e23a873062	9fb31b76-250b-402b-81e5-56d1d2d21dd6	8591adf5-04e8-4de2-8f24-e4125a7f4df1	5	File	Slide 6	a6808e3b-e613-4173-a64f-f95b876492f1	D. Power (P)\nElectrical power is the rate at which electrical energy is used or converted by a device or circuit.\nSymbol: P\nUnit: Watt (W)\nFormula:\nP = V × I\nExample:\nIf a device operates at 12 V and draws 2 A:\nP = 12 V × 2 A\vP = 24 W
636793e5-0b20-4d6f-a0b0-9076338ed604	69c54389-1873-4de2-b394-b7e23a873062	9fb31b76-250b-402b-81e5-56d1d2d21dd6	8591adf5-04e8-4de2-8f24-e4125a7f4df1	6	File	Slide 7	a6808e3b-e613-4173-a64f-f95b876492f1	II. Direct Current (DC): Concepts and Applications\nWhat is Direct Current (DC)?\nDirect Current (DC) is the flow of electric charge in one direction and is the primary operating power used by many electronic and digital devices.\nIn a basic DC circuit, the voltage maintains a fixed polarity, meaning the positive and negative terminals remain in the same positions.\nA battery is a common source of DC power.\nWhy is DC Important in Electronics?\nDC is very important because most electronic and digital devices operate using DC voltage.\nDevices such as Microcontrollers, Sensors and Alarm systems usually require stable, low-voltage DC power to operate properly.
b9fd9b62-0d0f-444b-a5ac-009619741f35	69c54389-1873-4de2-b394-b7e23a873062	9fb31b76-250b-402b-81e5-56d1d2d21dd6	8591adf5-04e8-4de2-8f24-e4125a7f4df1	7	File	Slide 8	a6808e3b-e613-4173-a64f-f95b876492f1	DC in Everyday IT and Electronic Devices\nEven though the electrical outlet in a building supplies AC (Alternating Current), many electronic devices actually operate internally using DC.\nA power adapter converts the AC from the outlet into the DC voltage required by the device.\nExample:\nAC from outlet → Power supply/adapter → DC → Electronic circuit
ec88a78b-8915-4b59-b4ff-a61e43923897	69c54389-1873-4de2-b394-b7e23a873062	9fb31b76-250b-402b-81e5-56d1d2d21dd6	8591adf5-04e8-4de2-8f24-e4125a7f4df1	8	File	Slide 9	a6808e3b-e613-4173-a64f-f95b876492f1	III. Alternating Current (AC): Terminology, Waveforms, Period, and Frequency\nWhat is Alternating Current (AC)?\nAlternating Current (AC) is electric current that periodically changes direction.\nUnlike DC, which flows in one direction, AC reverses direction repeatedly over time.\nIn an AC circuit, the voltage and current change continuously with time.\nWhy is AC Important in Electronics?\nAlternating Current (AC) is important because it is the main form of electrical power supplied by utility systems and wall outlets. Many electronic devices receive AC power first, which is then converted into DC for the electronic circuits inside the device.
d6f8db10-7a03-465d-8996-a2ca9581c262	69c54389-1873-4de2-b394-b7e23a873062	9fb31b76-250b-402b-81e5-56d1d2d21dd6	8591adf5-04e8-4de2-8f24-e4125a7f4df1	9	File	Slide 10	a6808e3b-e613-4173-a64f-f95b876492f1	AC in Everyday IT and Electronic Devices\nAC is commonly encountered in IT and electronic systems through wall outlets, power supplies, adapters, and backup power systems.\nCommon examples include:\nDesktop computers – Receive AC from the wall outlet, which the power supply converts into different DC voltages.\nLaptop chargers – Receive AC and convert it into DC for the laptop and battery.\nPrinters and other IT equipment – Use internal power supplies to convert AC into the DC voltages required by their electronic circuits.
0eeae965-5a9b-4973-90af-088ed1d9a112	69c54389-1873-4de2-b394-b7e23a873062	9fb31b76-250b-402b-81e5-56d1d2d21dd6	8591adf5-04e8-4de2-8f24-e4125a7f4df1	11	File	Slide 12	a6808e3b-e613-4173-a64f-f95b876492f1	Why Are Both AC and DC Used?\nAC and DC are not competing types of electricity where one is simply better. They are used for different purposes in an electrical system.\n1. AC for Power Distribution\nAC is widely used to deliver electrical power from the utility system to buildings and equipment.\nOne major advantage of AC is that transformers can easily increase or decrease AC voltage.\n2. AC is Converted to DC\nMany electronic devices cannot operate directly from the AC supplied by a wall outlet.\nA power supply or adapter converts AC into the DC voltage required by the device.\n3. DC Powers Electronic Circuits\nOnce AC has been converted to DC, the DC supply is used by many electronic circuits and devices.
e4e805f1-8807-438f-a316-ab81dc5eb109	69c54389-1873-4de2-b394-b7e23a873062	9fb31b76-250b-402b-81e5-56d1d2d21dd6	8591adf5-04e8-4de2-8f24-e4125a7f4df1	12	File	Slide 13	a6808e3b-e613-4173-a64f-f95b876492f1	END OF WEEK 2 LESSON
