baf90e3a-43f5-4b94-ac33-253f5ac50c6c	eedb9551-c449-4a57-8961-30a2409b0151	1	published	11	Lesson 1	<p><strong>The concept of power applies across several key domains:</strong></p><p><strong>1. Physics and Engineering</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Definition:</strong> Power measures how quickly energy is expended or work is performed (P = W / t, where W is work and t is time).</p></li><li><p><strong>Units:</strong> Measured in <strong>watts (W)</strong>, which equal one joule per second (1 W = 1 J/s).</p></li><li><p><strong>Mechanical Power:</strong> Calculated as the product of force and velocity (P = F ⋅ v) or torque and angular speed (P = τ ⋅ ω).</p></li><li><p><strong>Electrical Power:</strong> Calculated as the product of voltage and current (P = V ⋅ I). [<a href="https://study.com/learn/lesson/video/power-formula-equation-examples.html" rel="noopener noreferrer">1</a>, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/shorts/3d4_FshrKTo" rel="noopener noreferrer">2</a>, <a href="https://www.britannica.com/science/power-physics" rel="noopener noreferrer">3</a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_(physics)" rel="noopener noreferrer">4</a>]</p></li></ul><p><strong>2. Social Sciences and Politics</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Definition:</strong> Power is the capacity or ability to influence, direct, dominate, or impact the behavior, beliefs, and actions of others—even in the face of resistance. [<a href="https://www.open.edu/openlearncreate/mod/book/view.php?id=193963&amp;chapterid=28064" rel="noopener noreferrer">1</a>, <a href="https://www.britannica.com/topic/power-political-and-social-science" rel="noopener noreferrer">2</a>]</p></li><li><p><strong>Authority vs. Power:</strong> Authority is power that carries formal legitimacy or institutional backing, whereas power can be exercised without official sanction. [<a href="https://www.britannica.com/topic/power-political-and-social-science" rel="noopener noreferrer">1</a>]</p></li><li><p><strong>Types:</strong> Sociologists like Max Weber and modern frameworks categorize power into visible, hidden, and invisible dynamics, as well as coercion or persuasion. You can read more about how these concepts function via <a href="https://www.britannica.com/topic/power-political-and-social-science" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong><u>Encyclopaedia Britannica</u></strong></a>. [<a href="https://www.open.edu/openlearncreate/mod/book/view.php?id=193963&amp;chapterid=28064" rel="noopener noreferrer">1</a>, <a href="https://www.britannica.com/topic/power-political-and-social-science" rel="noopener noreferrer">2</a>]</p></li></ul><p><strong>3. Mathematics</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Definition:</strong> Power (or exponentiation) indicates how many times a base number is multiplied by itself (e.g., \\(x^{n}\\)). [<a href="https://dictionary.cambridge.org/us/dictionary/english/power" rel="noopener noreferrer">1</a>]</p></li></ul><p>Would you like to dive deeper into <strong>physics calculations (formulas and examples)</strong> or explore <strong>political and social theories of power</strong>?</p>	\N	\N		\N	d5c556b4-c712-467f-b29e-2822d878f345	d5c556b4-c712-467f-b29e-2822d878f345	2026-10-08 10:40:13.137696+08	2026-10-08 10:43:53.98647+08	2026-10-08 10:43:53.797969+08	indexed
